            0.451,
            0.087,
            0.882,
            0.157
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. With the help of a needle, carefully transfer a little mould onto a microscope slide. Under the guidance of your teacher, add cotton blue stain — a coloured dye to help see it better."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.451,
            0.159,
            0.882,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. Observe the mould under the microscope and draw its diagram based on your observations."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.451,
            0.213,
            0.882,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Compare",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the diagram you have drawn with Fig. 11.8 and share your observations with your classmates."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.072,
            0.246,
            0.138,
            0.3
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.072,
                0.246,
                0.138,
                0.3
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.265,
            0.45,
            0.284
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Bridging Science and Society"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.289,
            0.847,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Moulds on bread may look unpleasant but fungi benefit society greatly. Fungi grow very fast by spore formation and degrade organic wastes and pollutants. Fungi also play an important role in removal of heavy metals from industrial wastes. Many antibiotics are derived from fungi (penicillin and amoxicillin), saving countless lives from bacterial infections. Do you know any fungus that can degrade plastic?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.061,
            0.422,
            0.121,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.061,
                0.422,
                0.121,
                0.457
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.419,
            0.239,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.076,
            0.463,
            0.295,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We kept the moist chamber warm (25 – 35 °C) because spores of mould present in the air need warmth and moisture to grow on bread or roti. Lower temperatures slow or stop their reproduction. This is why, we refrigerate perishable food. Before refrigerators became common about 100 years ago, fresh food lasted only 1 – 2 days. Refrigeration (including deep freezing) revolutionised food habits, enabling yearround availability of fruits, vegetables, and dairy products, while preventing their spoilage from moulds and bacteria."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.328,
            0.392,
            0.805,
            0.442
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Do you observe thread-like structures with a round sac at the tip? Do you also see tiny round structure (spores) inside the sac? Where did the mould on your"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.81,
            0.396,
            0.881,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Grade 8",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Curiosity "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 2",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.327,
            0.441,
            0.889,
            0.578
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "bread slice come from? It was not present when the bread was fresh. You may have observed fungi growing on rotten fruits or while preparing manure. In these cases, fungi grew due to spores present in the air. Similarly, in the case of bread, the mould grew from the spores already present in the air, which settled on the moist bread and began reproducing rapidly. Spores are formed in a sac-like structure (Fig. 11.8a) or on a swollen vesicle on a long strip of fungal hyphae (Fig. 11.8b)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.326,
            0.58,
            0.889,
            0.649
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Spores are produced in huge numbers (millions from one mould colony). Spores are lightweight, usually single-celled and float easily through air currents, waiting for moisture and nutrients to germinate quickly into a new individual."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.327,
            0.651,
            0.89,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In all the organisms studied so far in this chapter, the central process behind asexual reproduction is mitosis, a type of cell division that produces two daughter cells, each having the same number of chromosomes identical to the parent cell. Because of this, the offspring produced are genetically identical to the parent and are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "clones",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". This method is fast and helps organisms increase their population quickly, especially when environmental conditions are favourable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.329,
            0.797,
            0.66,
            0.818
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.2 Sexual Reproduction"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.327,
            0.821,
            0.891,
            0.939
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sexual reproduction involves two parents in the formation of a new individual. This means both parents contribute to the genetic material of the offspring. However, if each generation were to receive the full set of chromosomes from both the parents, the chromosome number would double in every generation. This biological problem is solved by a special type of cell division known as meiosis."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
